{
  "id": "chassis",
  "name": "Chassis / Unibody Structure",
  "summary": "The Gen 6 Camaro is a steel unibody (GM Alpha-derived) with bolt-on front and rear suspension cradles. GM quotes the coupe body as 28% stiffer and 133 lb lighter than Gen 5. A road-race build starts from a stripped coupe shell that is measured, stitch welded at critical seams, reinforced at cradle and cage mounting points, re-sealed and painted before lines, suspension and drivetrain are installed.",
  "overview": "The body-in-white carries all loads: the front cradle (engine, front lower control arms, radius rods, steering rack, front sway bar) bolts up into the two front frame rails; the MacPherson struts load the front strut towers directly; the rear cradle (differential, all five rear links, rear sway bar) bolts to the rear rails through four isolating bushings and is braced fore and aft by bolted cradle braces; the rear shocks load the rear shock pads while the rear coil springs react against body spring seats above the rear lower control arms.\n\nFor racing, the main structural tasks are: (1) measure the shell against GM underbody dimension data and record cradle-mount and strut-tower positions; (2) strip deadener/sealer at the seams to be welded; (3) stitch weld the strut towers, front rail flanges, floor-to-rocker, tunnel and rear rail/shock tower seams; (4) add doubler plates at cradle mounts (especially if cradles will be solid-mounted) and at all cage feet; (5) integrate the roll cage into towers, pillars and rockers where the rules allow; (6) re-seal, prime, paint and cavity-wax the shell.\n\nRules: NASA CCR and SCCA GCR control cage design, plate sizes, tow-point requirements, fuel-cell bulkheads and (by class) what seam welding or cage tie-ins are allowed - read the class rules before cutting or welding. Common failure points on hard-driven Gen 6 cars are cracked paint/seams at the strut towers, spun captured nuts at cradle mounts, and crushed rocker pinch welds from careless jacking.\n\nAll cradle and many suspension fasteners on this platform are torque-to-yield (torque + angle) and single-use per GM; chase every captured nut and plan on new fasteners for final assembly.",
  "buildNotes": [
    "Buy/choose a coupe shell; verify VIN plates/documentation for registration and tech.",
    "Mount on rotisserie/jig using cradle mounting points; record baseline measurements (cradle diagonals, tower-to-tower, rear shock pad positions).",
    "Strip interior, deadener, sealer at weld zones; remove all lines and harnesses.",
    "Stitch weld front structure, underbody and rear structure in a skip pattern; re-measure.",
    "Weld cradle-mount doublers, strut tower reinforcements, jacking points, tabs, and cage landing plates; fit the cage (safety system).",
    "Chase all captured nuts; install rivnuts along planned line/harness routes.",
    "Epoxy prime, seam seal, paint, cavity wax, light chip coating; mask datum holes and ground pads.",
    "Proceed to phase 2 (fuel cell, hard lines) then phase 3 (cradles, suspension, steering)."
  ],
